// @summary:   binary -> reflected Gray code, bit 0 least significant
// @technique: plain
// @source:    reflected binary Gray code
// @confirmed: reproduced by tests/jana2014/reference/gray_code_c.py, an independent Python implementation written from the algorithm (only the inputs are transcribed from main); it agrees on bits
// @keep:      bits
// @expect: bits[4] = {1, 1, 1, 1}
//
// Reflected binary Gray code — no-I/O variant (verified-oracle twin of
// tests/jana2014_in_out/programs/gray_code.ja).
//
// The Gray encoding g[i] = b[i] XOR b[i+1] is a clean reversible bijection on
// bit vectors, done in place with one `^=` per bit; `uncall gray` decodes.
// Input is baked into main and the encoded vector is left in the store, so
// tests/jana2014/test_vjanus_corpus.py diffs the Coq-extracted `run` against
// PyJanus on it. Here bits = 1010 (b3,b1 set) -> Gray 1111.

procedure gray(int bits[])
    iterate int i = 0 to 2
        bits[i] ^= bits[i+1]
    end

procedure main()
    int bits[4]
    bits[3] += 1
    bits[1] += 1
    call gray(bits)
